\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{Coefficient rows.} One row per coefficient, on the log-hazard scale
for a Cox model and on the scale of the survival time (its log for the
log-scale distributions) for \code{survreg}. Under \code{exponentiate = TRUE},
Cox models give hazard ratios and the log-scale distributions of
\code{survreg} give time ratios. Identity-scale distributions are left
untouched. The distribution parameters of a \code{flexsurvreg} fit render
as a block of their own.

\strong{Absolute effects.} The RMST difference and the risk difference are
computed by g-computation (regression standardization): the survival
curve of every subject is predicted under each level of the predictor,
the other covariates kept as observed, the curves are averaged, and the
two standardized curves are integrated up to \code{tau} (RMST) or read at
\code{at_time} and complemented (risk). Their standard errors and intervals
come from a nonparametric bootstrap of \code{boot_n} replicates. For
\code{coxph}, the columns are defined for right-censored single-record fits.
\code{strata()} is supported: standardization keeps each subject's own
stratum baseline, and the note says so. For \code{survreg}, the closed-form
curves of the AFT model are standardized directly.

\strong{Fit statistics.} By default, \code{n}, the number of events and \code{AIC} for
Cox fits; see \emph{Fit statistics} below.
}

