\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

The coefficients are tested by Wald z. A robust \code{vcov} is the one
cluster estimator of each class:
\itemize{
\item \code{coxph}: \code{"CR0"}, the Lin-Wei grouped-dfbeta sandwich, the
variance of \code{coxph(cluster = )}.
\item \code{cph}: \code{"CR0"}, from \code{\link[rms:robcov]{rms::robcov()}}, which needs the fit's
\verb{x = TRUE, y = TRUE}.
\item \code{survreg}: \code{"CR1"}, from \code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}}: the CR0 sandwich
scaled by \eqn{G/(G-1)}{G/(G-1)}, \code{G} the number of clusters.
}
The Lin-Wei and \code{robcov()} sandwiches have no small-sample factor,
hence \code{"CR0"}; the other \verb{CR*} tokens are refused rather than mapped.
For a censored \code{coxph} fit, \code{cluster} has one entry per subject.

Hierarchical comparison (\code{nested = TRUE}). Nested Cox fits, and nested
\code{survreg} fits, are compared by likelihood-ratio test,
\code{c("lrt_change", "p_change")}. Under \code{"CR0"} (\code{coxph}, \code{cph}) or
\code{"CR1"} (\code{survreg}), and for \code{survreg(robust = TRUE)}, the change test
is the Wald chi-square of the added coefficients on the robust matrix.
A Cox model without a coefficient (\code{~ 1}, or \code{strata()} alone) can be
the first model of the hierarchy: its column shows the fit statistics,
and the next model its likelihood-ratio test against it. Alone, it is
refused, since it has no row to show. See \emph{Hierarchical (nested) model
comparison} in \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

Cox fits default to \code{c("nobs", "n_events", "aic")}: the
number of events is reported beside \code{n}. \code{survreg} and \code{flexsurvreg}
fits default to \code{c("nobs", "aic")}. On
request:
\itemize{
\item \code{"concordance"}: Harrell's C of a \code{coxph} fit, the value
\code{summary()} reports. When the row is shown, the concordance leaves
the note, which otherwise gives it with its standard error. \code{cph}
fits have no concordance row or note.
\item \code{"sigma"}: for \code{survreg}, the scale \eqn{\sigma}{sigma} of the
model, blank when strata give one scale per stratum. Asked for in a
table where every \code{survreg} fit is stratified, it is refused. See
\link{table_regression_tokens}.
}
}

\section{Tied event times and the survival estimands}{

A Cox model has to decide what to do when several events share the same
time, and \code{survival::coxph()} decides with \code{ties = "efron"} by default.
The absolute estimands do not make a second decision: the baseline
hazard behind the RMST-difference and risk-difference columns follows
the tie-handling convention of the fit, as \code{\link[survival:survfit]{survival::survfit()}} and
\code{\link[survival:basehaz]{survival::basehaz()}} do. A \code{ties = "breslow"} fit gives a Breslow
baseline; the default Efron fit gives an Efron baseline. The
hazard-ratio column and the RMST column thus come from the same
likelihood. To change the convention, change the fit.

The number of ties alone does not say whether the choice matters. What
governs it is the fraction of tied events within the risk set, together
with the effect size and the spread of the covariates: a large risk set
dilutes a tied block, a small one (fine strata, matched or nested
case-control designs) does not. A file with hundreds of tied days can
be insensitive to the convention while a small stratified one with a
handful of ties is not.

The approximations are all biased at finite sample size and agree
asymptotically. Efron's has a much smaller small-sample bias
(Hertz-Picciotto & Rockhill 1997), which makes it the default. Efron
(1977), after Peto, expected the choice to make little difference in
practice.
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item The AME columns for \code{coxph} and \code{cph} (see \emph{Engines}), and for
\code{flexsurvreg}.
\item The RMST and risk-difference columns for \code{cph} and
\code{flexsurvreg}, for a \code{coxph} fit with time-transform \code{tt()} terms,
for a stratified \code{survreg} fit, whose per-stratum scale
parameters are not standardized (refit without \code{strata()}), and
for a \code{coxph} fit with a \code{frailty()} term, whose random effect the
estimand would ignore.
\item The objects that inherit from \code{coxph} or \code{survreg} without
being that model: \code{survival::clogit()} fits, multi-state \code{coxph}
fits, \code{coxph} fits with a \code{pspline()} or a non-sparse \code{frailty()}
term, and \code{survey::svysurvreg()} fits.
\item \code{"rmst"} columns without \code{tau}, and \code{"risk_diff"} columns
without \code{at_time}.
\item \verb{HC*}, \code{"bootstrap"} and \code{"jackknife"} for every class, and
the \verb{CR*} tokens other than the one estimator of the class.
\item \code{standardized} for every class of this family.
}
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("survival", quietly = TRUE)) {
  library(survival)
  lung$sex <- factor(lung$sex, labels = c("Male", "Female"))
  fit <- coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ age + sex, data = lung)
  print(table_regression(fit, exponentiate = TRUE))

  # RMST difference over the first year, from 200 bootstrap replicates.
  set.seed(1)
  print(table_regression(fit, show_columns = c("b", "rmst", "rmst_ci"),
                         tau = 365, boot_n = 200))
}
}

